Payments, Transfers & Cutoff Times
7. Future-dated payments and standing orders
A future-dated payment is executed on a date chosen by the customer. A standing order is a repeated future-dated payment. The customer must ensure there is enough available balance before the payment is due.
If there is not enough available balance at the processing time, Aurora Bank may:
- reject the payment;
- retry the payment later the same day;
- create or increase an arranged overdraft if available and permitted;
- create an unarranged overdraft only where permitted by product terms.
Rejected-payment fees may apply. See [Fees, Interest & Foreign Exchange](06_fees_interest_and_exchange_rates.md).

Current & Savings Accounts Terms
12. Account restrictions
Aurora Bank may restrict an account where:
- security is at risk;
- suspicious or unauthorized activity is detected;
- the customer has not provided required information;
- a court order, tax order, enforcement order, sanctions requirement, or legal notice applies;
- the account is being used outside product terms;
- a dispute between joint account holders cannot be resolved;
- the customer is insolvent or subject to a legal process.
We will inform the customer where we are legally allowed to do so. Restrictions can affect cards, online banking, payments, cash withdrawals, statements, and account closure.

Current & Savings Accounts Terms
2. Current account core services
A current account normally allows the customer to:
- receive salary, pension, benefits, refunds, and other incoming payments;
- make SEPA credit transfers and, where available, instant payments;
- set up standing orders and direct debits;
- use a debit card for purchases, cash withdrawals, and online payments;
- use mobile wallet services where the device and card are eligible;
- view balances, statements, and payment confirmations;
- send secure messages to Aurora Bank;
- request account documents such as balance confirmations or interest certificates.
A current account is not a safe-deposit service, investment product, escrow arrangement, or business merchant-processing account.

Account Closure, Inactivity, Estates & Power of Attorney
2. Closure restrictions
Aurora Bank may refuse or delay closure where:
- the account has a negative balance;
- there are pending card transactions, payment disputes, or pre-authorizations;
- a court order, tax order, enforcement order, or sanctions restriction applies;
- fraud, money laundering, scam, or complaint investigation is ongoing;
- a fixed-term product has not matured;
- a joint account holder has not consented where required;
- legal authority for a representative is incomplete;
- funds are subject to estate, insolvency, or guardianship processes.
We will explain the reason where legally allowed.

Account Closure, Inactivity, Estates & Power of Attorney
3. Bank-initiated closure
Aurora Bank may close an account by giving notice under the account terms. We may close immediately or restrict services where required because of:
- fraud or suspected fraud;
- threatening or abusive conduct;
- illegal or prohibited account use;
- sanctions or legal requirements;
- false information;
- account used for business activity contrary to personal-account terms;
- failure to provide required information;
- serious breach of terms;
- risk to bank systems or other customers.
Where possible, remaining funds will be returned to the customer after legal and security checks.

